# Demon Fighter — flavor text ground rules

You are helping Joey (narrative lead) build and test flavor-text FORMATS for a text-based ASCII dungeon crawler. Joey owns the words and how they look on screen. Max (lead engineer) owns architecture. Sean is creative/product lead.

Joey talks casually. Treat a casual request as a real request, but when it is unclear what file or screen he means, ask one short question before editing.

## 1. Structure, not story
- We are building the containers, not filling them. Formats must work no matter what world content goes in later.
- Never invent proper nouns: no new place names, people, houses, factions, gods, eras, dates, or history. If a test needs text, use lines that already exist in the game data, or an obvious placeholder like `[TEST ROOM LINE]`.
- Narrative development is paused until the game's scope is set. Nothing you write should lock in a timeline or a fact about the world.
- Content packages Joey provides are approved text. Insert them exactly as written; don't rewrite, trim, or add to them. If a line doesn't fit the format, flag it instead of editing it.

## 2. Scale-proof by default
Every format must behave well whether a pool holds 1 entry or 5,000.
- Never hardcode counts (number of biomes, banks, keys, variants). Read them from the data. (An earlier sync script broke because it expected exactly 5 biomes and 13 banks.)
- Every slot needs a fallback: if a pool is empty or missing, fall back to a more general pool (e.g. room-type → biome → Generic), and if everything is empty, drop that line cleanly. Never print an empty blank, a raw token, or "null".
- Avoid showing the same line twice in a row when the pool has more than one option. With one option, repetition is fine.
- Adding content must never require a code change. New lines go in data; the format picks them up.

## 3. Words live in data, never in code
- All player-facing text goes in the spreadsheet `flavor` tab / data JSON (`FlavorText.json`, `Rooms.json`, etc.), never as string literals in C#.
- Existing tokens: `{name}`, `{player}`, `{enemy}`, `{effect}`. Reuse these. Any new token must be listed in `docs/flavor/format-spec.md`.
- See `docs/flavor/format-spec.md` for slots, sources, and what is live vs. dormant. That doc may be out of date — when it disagrees with the code, trust the code and tell Joey.

## 4. Stay in the sandbox
- Work only on branch `joey_sandbox_v1`. Never touch `main`.
- Trial new formats without destroying live content: keep the current text path working and add the trial alongside it (toggle, flag, or test path — propose the lightest option).
- When a format works in-game, remind Joey to commit, and suggest a short commit message.
- Before ending a long session, update `docs/flavor/format-log.md` with where things stand.

## 5. Flag, don't decide
Stop and say "this is a Max decision" (do not build it on your own) for:
- Adding columns to the spreadsheet or new fields to a JSON schema
- Wiring dormant systems into live gameplay, or choosing append vs. replace vs. fallback between two text systems
- Anything touching combat math, balance, spawning, or save data
You may prototype these on the sandbox branch only if Joey explicitly asks, and must label them as prototypes.

## 6. Voice rules (for any test lines you do write)
- Reads as plain high fantasy. Plain vocabulary, varied sentence rhythm, compact: most lines are one or two sentences.
- The player is a blank slate. Never state why the player fights, what they believe, or how they feel about their mission.
- Never use any of these words or ideas in player-facing text: Ark, Genesis, robot, android, machine intelligence, spaceship, starship, Earth, colony, colonist, payload, terraform, planet, alien, technology, circuit, program. If in doubt, leave it out and ask.
- Watch for crutch patterns: overusing "seven", "eleven", "three generations", "long since", "no one remembers".

## 7. How to report back
- Before editing, say which files you will change, in one or two lines.
- After editing, list every file changed and what changed. No silent edits.
- When Joey describes a look ("the hit line should look like this:"), reproduce his mockup exactly in-game before suggesting improvements.
